<div class="ch-hdr"><div class="chnum">Chapter 5 · 2003&ndash;2009</div><h2>The Cultural Soundtrack</h2></div>
<div class="read-sec">
<div class="sec-ttl">Music That Defined an Era</div>
<div class="rtxt">
<p>The later 2000s were loud with pop transformation. ${t('Beyoncé')} moved from Destiny's Child into solo superstardom, and songs like "Crazy in Love" with ${t('Jay-Z')} helped establish her as a defining performer of the era. By 2008, "Single Ladies" became not just a song but a visual and dance-language event that spread across platforms and culture.</p>
<p>${t('Jay-Z')} stood at the crossroads of rap, business, fashion, sports culture, and celebrity power. Hip-hop was no longer treated as a marginal youth form; it was central to American popular culture.</p>
<p>${t('Taylor Swift')} emerged in country-pop as a teenage songwriter whose early appeal rested on diary-like lyrics, romance, insecurity, and high-school feeling before her later full turn into global pop. ${t('Lady Gaga')} broke through at the end of the decade with dance-pop, theatrical fashion, club culture, and a deliberately constructed outsider-glam image. Her late-2000s rise pointed toward a new pop world where music, fashion, video, internet fandom, and identity all fused into a single cultural experience.</p>
</div></div>

<div class="img-wrap"><img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," style="width:100%;display:block;border-radius:4px 4px 0 0" alt="Beyoncé's "Single Ladies (Put a Ring on It)" video (2008) became an instant cultural landmark"><div class="img-cap">Beyoncé's "Single Ladies (Put a Ring on It)" video (2008) became an instant cultural landmark</div></div><div class="qck" id="q9">
<div class="qt">Quick Check</div>
<div class="qq">What was significant about hip-hop's place in American culture during the later 2000s?</div>
<div class="qopt" onclick="aQC('q9',this,false)"><span class="qlet">A</span>It was popular only in large coastal cities like New York and Los Angeles</div>
<div class="qopt" onclick="aQC('q9',this,true)"><span class="qlet">B</span>It became central to American popular culture, no longer treated as a marginal youth form</div>
<div class="qopt" onclick="aQC('q9',this,false)"><span class="qlet">C</span>It declined significantly in popularity compared to the 1990s</div>
<div class="qfb" id="q9-fb"></div></div>

<div class="read-sec">
<div class="sec-ttl">The Participatory Culture Shift</div>
<div class="rtxt">
<p>The decade's television and music trends shared a pattern: the audience was becoming part of the machine. Fans voted on ${t('American Idol')}. Viewers discussed <em>Lost</em> theories online. ${t('YouTube')} users uploaded covers, clips, jokes, dances, news, and arguments. ${t('MySpace')} helped bands build audiences. ${t('Twitter')} made public conversation faster and harsher. ${t('Texting','texting')} changed friendship. Facebook changed identity. The ${t('iPhone')} made all of it portable.</p>
<p>This was the promise and the curse of the later 2000s. Culture became more participatory, more immediate, more democratic in some ways. More people could publish, respond, remix, organize, and be seen. But the same systems also made fame unstable, privacy fragile, cruelty scalable, and attention easier to capture.</p>
<p>So the later 2000s close with two scenes laid over each other. In one, Obama speaks before a crowd and a country imagines a new chapter. In the other, banks fail, wars drag on, floodwater memories remain, comment sections burn, phones glow in every hand, and the old media world begins to sink beneath the rising tide of the ${t('social media')} age.</p>
</div></div>

<div class="rfl">
<div class="rt">Reflect &amp; Connect</div>
<div class="rq">The text says culture became "more participatory" but also made "cruelty more scalable." What does it mean for cruelty to be "scalable"? Why might the internet allow unkindness to spread in ways it couldn't before?</div></div>

<div class="rfl blue">
<div class="rt">Chapter Review: The Big Picture</div>
<div class="rq">The 2000s began with fear of terrorism and ended with fear of economic collapse — and throughout, a new digital world was being built. In your own words: what was the single most important change of the later 2000s, and why?</div></div>

<div class="cont"><button class="ibtn green" onclick="goFwd()">Take the Knowledge Check ▶</button></div>`;
}

function buildQuiz(){
